Assistant: Water temperature is your first line of defense against Legionella in your home's plumbing. These bacteria thrive in that lukewarm zone between 77-108°F — exactly where many water heaters are mistakenly set to save energy. The best approach is to keeping your water heater at 140°F (60°C) minimum, then using mixing valves at each tap to prevent scalding. Think of it as creating an hostile environment throughout your entire system. Cold water should stay genuinely cold (below 68°F), and hot water should stay properly hot. Those neglected guest bathroom showers or that utility sink in the basement? They're creating perfect breeding grounds when water sits at room temperature. Make it a practice to flush any tap that hasn't been used for a week — let the hot water run until it's truly hot, then flush with cold. Consider installing a recirculation pump if you have long pipe runs. Your water system should maintain clear thermal boundaries, just like keeping raw and cooked foods separate in your kitchen.

